# Line-ending policy.
#
# This matters for correctness, not just tidiness: a shell script committed with
# CRLF line endings fails on Linux/macOS with
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# because the kernel treats the trailing CR as part of the interpreter path. The
# scripts in this repository are run from Git Bash on Windows, from Linux, and
# from macOS, so they are pinned to LF.
#
# `text=auto` normalises everything else to LF in the repository, and git checks
# out LF on all platforms. Windows editors handle LF fine; the binaries are
# explicitly excluded so nothing rewrites them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell and Python: LF is mandatory (see above).
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.cdf           text eol=lf

# PowerShell and batch: LF works, and keeping the repo uniform avoids the
# mixed-ending diffs that come from per-platform checkout rules.
*.ps1           text eol=lf
*.bat           text eol=lf

# Prose and data: LF.
*.md            text eol=lf
*.MD            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.xml           text eol=lf

# Never touch these. `-text` disables any conversion, including CRLF mangling on
# checkout, which would corrupt them.
*.bin           -text
*.img           -text
*.dat           -text
*.br            -text
*.gz            -text
*.so            -text
*.apk           -text
*.jar           -text
*.der           -text
*.cat           -text
*.msi           -text
*.zip           -text
*.pfx           -text
